Not sure on genetic matching, but for matching
in general if you keep the number of control observations matched the
same (i.e,. j to k matching where j and k stay fixed over
observations), then you don't need weights.  



Also, the weights, if necessary (such as in CEM), are for computing the
quantity of interest, not necessarily for extrapolating.  So you can
fit a model without weights and then use the weights only when
aggregating into your quantity of interest.



